The training medians show that MAPbI3 n-i-p cells reach 18–21 % only when four physical requirements are met simultaneously: (i) an electron-selective contact whose conduction-band edge lies 0.1–0.4 eV below that of MAPbI3 and that also passivates interfacial traps (TiO2-c/SnO2-np bilayers, TiO2-c/PCBM, SnO2-np, or modified C60), (ii) a hole-selective contact with HOMO aligned to the perovskite valence band and sufficient conductivity (Spiro-MeOTAD, PTAA or the closely related PCDTBT), (iii) a chemically inert, high-work-function back contact (Au or MoO3/Au), and (iv) an absorber process capable of producing compact, low-defect films (spin-coating, spin + CBD/dipp, or vacuum co-evaporation). Flexible substrates, p-type “ETLs”, undoped poor-mobility HTLs (P3HT, none) and reactive Ag contacts without interlayers systematically sit 3–8 points lower, consistent with increased recombination, extraction barriers and series resistance.

Applying these constraints to the pool isolates five stacks whose layer identities and processing routes are essentially identical to the highest-training medians:

- c064 reproduces the 20.85 % champion ETL/HTL pair on FTO, merely substituting the more inert Au contact;
- c005 is the 19.35 % device with only a side-chain variant of the same polymer HTL;
- c068 is the 18.19 % SnO2-np/PTAA cell with an extra thin TiO2-np overlayer and Au instead of Ag;
- c017 is the 17.9 % co-evaporated C60/TaTm:F6-TCNNQ cell (Ag versus Au);
- c025 combines a SnO2-c/TiO2-np bilayer (analogous to the 16.5–16.7 % SnO2/TiO2 devices) with a two-step CBD absorber growth that the training set already associates with 17–18 % films.

All other candidates violate at least one of the four requirements and are therefore expected to lie below this group.

Final: c064, c005, c068, c017, c025
